{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"command -v jq; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '35,145p'\"","aggregated_output":"/usr/bin/jq\n    35\t\n    36\t\\section{Introduction}\n    37\tScientific prediction and scientific inference solve different problems. A predictor can be highly useful for assigning scores to observations while remaining biased for a population average. Conversely, a small labeled sample can support an unbiased mean estimate but an imprecise confidence interval. Prediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a large sample of predictions with a labeled correction, retaining the inferential target rather than treating predictions as ground truth \\citep{PPI}. Its efficiency depends on the relationship between the labels and the available scores, not simply on the availability of a large prediction table.\n    38\t\n    39\tWe investigate the fixed-resource version of this problem. Within each comparison, every method receives exactly the same labeled outcomes, corresponding scalar predictions, and unlabeled predictions. There is no new label acquisition, predictor retraining, or enlarged prediction pool. The target is the population mean. This setting isolates a practical question: can postprocessing an existing score yield narrower intervals than both the original PPI correction and the power-tuned PPI++ reference \\citep{PPIPlus}, without mistaking an empirical width reduction for a guarantee of validity?\n    40\t\n    41\tThe method studied here learns a small control function of the frozen score. It uses outer held-out folds for the mean correction and inner folds to compare zero, affine, and nonlinear calibrations. Its variance estimate centers labeled residuals within folds and evaluates the weighted sum of all fold controls on the single shared unlabeled pool. The construction is motivated by an elementary distinction: an affine rescaling can correct a score's scale, but cannot in general recover a nonlinear conditional mean. Nevertheless, estimating that nonlinear relationship from scarce labels can consume the potential gain. Oracle headroom is therefore a rationale to investigate, not evidence of realized benefit.\n    42\t\n    43\tOur contribution is a delimited construction and matched empirical assessment. On the empirical gene-expression source, the frozen method is narrower than label-only inference, PPI, and PPI++. Against PPI++, however, none of the other evaluated conditions benefits. Across the assessment, measured coverage is compatible with nominal coverage but too uncertain to establish it. We make the unfavorable comparisons part of the central result rather than treating them as peripheral failures.\n    44\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/summary]]\n    45\t\n    46\tThe study thus contributes an inspectable score-only implementation, an explanation of its variance target and additive-offset invariance, and a comparison that separates efficiency, uncertainty, and validity. No controlled component ablation is available, so we do not attribute the empirical-source gain specifically to nonlinear calibration or to studentization. The operative conclusion is conditional: recalibration can help on the measured source, while improving over an already tuned reference is substantially harder than improving over an unweighted correction.\n    47\t\n    48\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    49\t\\centering\n    50\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fixed_resource_overview.png}\n    51\t\\caption{Fixed-resource mean inference. The training boundary separates selection of a score control from the labels used for its held-out correction. Fold controls share one unlabeled sample; inference uses their weighted aggregate rather than independent copies of that sample. The returned asymptotic interval and its repeated-sampling assessment are distinct from an internal bounded construction that is not returned. No arrow denotes a validity certificate.}\n    52\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    53\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/program.py#L154-L305]]\n    54\t\\end{figure}\n    55\t\n    56\t\\section{Related Work}\n    57\t\\paragraph{Correction and efficiency.}\n    58\tPPI \\citep{PPI} supplies the prediction-plus-correction framework; PPI++ \\citep{PPIPlus} makes power tuning an essential comparator rather than a cosmetic implementation choice. The present study does not replace either framework with a new inferential target. It considers a restricted learned transformation of an externally frozen scalar score. Cross-prediction \\citep{CrossPPI} addresses inference when prediction itself is learned with the available labels. Here only the calibration is learned; the upstream predictor remains fixed. Cross-fitting and orthogonal correction also have a broader role in debiased inference \\citep{DML}, but that connection does not automatically verify assumptions for a particular selected calibration.\n    59\t\n    60\t\\paragraph{Controls and model-assisted means.}\n    61\tThe control-variate perspective \\citep{Glynn}, generalized difference estimation \\citep{Cassel}, and model-assisted sampling \\citep{Sarndal} motivate correcting a prediction-based mean with observed residuals. Semi-supervised mean inference explicitly studies how finite unlabeled information changes efficiency \\citep{Zhang}. Empirical efficiency maximization \\citep{Rubin} provides related motivation for selecting an adjustment by variance rather than prediction error alone. These connections explain why our inner objective contains both residual variability and a finite-unlabeled-sample penalty. They do not identify the present procedure as optimal over all estimators or all interval constructions.\n    62\t\n    63\t\\paragraph{Learning and inferential boundaries.}\n    64\tThe low-dimensional calibration uses ridge regularization \\citep{Hoerl} and elementary basis expansion \\citep{Hastie}. Held-out selection follows the general cross-validation principle \\citep{Stone}; dependence among reused training sets complicates uncertainty assessment \\citep{Nadeau}. Out-of-fold prediction also appears in stacked generalization and stacked regression \\citep{Wolpert,Breiman}, but our measured method selects one control family per outer fold rather than fitting a stack. For uncertainty reporting, exact binomial intervals \\citep{ClopperPearson} and the known pitfalls of normal binomial approximations \\citep{Brown} are particularly relevant to rare outcomes. Bounded concentration \\citep{Hoeffding,MaurerPontil} is conceptually different from the Student-based interval returned here. Keeping these distinctions explicit is necessary for interpreting a width advantage scientifically.\n    65\t\n    66\t\\section{Method}\n    67\t\\subsection{Problem setup and fixed resources}\n    68\tLet $(Y_i,P_i)_{i=1}^{n}$ be independent, identically distributed labeled pairs and $(U_j)_{j=1}^{N}$ an independent sample from the same marginal score distribution. The scalar score is $P=f(X)$ for an externally frozen predictor. We estimate $\\theta=\\E[Y]$. The inferential objective is a short interval $I$ satisfying $\\Pr\\{\\theta\\in I\\}\\geq 1-\\alpha$ under repeated sampling. Fixing resources means holding the realized inputs equal \\emph{across methods}, not asserting coverage conditional on the entire realized labeled sample. Informative label selection, predictor training on these labels, and a shifted unlabeled population require additional justification and are outside this setup.\n    69\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L12-L28]]\n    70\t\n    71\tFor any deterministic score control $g$ with finite second moments, consider\n    72\t\\begin{equation}\n    73\t \\widehat\\theta_g=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\{Y_i-g(P_i)\\}\n    74\t             +\\frac1N\\sum_{j=1}^Ng(U_j).\n    75\t \\label{eq:control}\n    76\t\\end{equation}\n    77\tThe correction removes the mean bias of $g$, so $g$ need not be an unbiased predictor. Setting $g=0$ gives the labeled mean, while $g(p)=p$ gives the unweighted PPI point estimate. A tuned linear multiple yields the corresponding scalar power-tuned family. Our rationale is to enlarge this control class slightly without spending additional observations or modifying $f$.\n    78\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L108-L143]]\n    79\t\n    80\t\\subsection{Training-only risk adaptation}\n    81\tThe implementation divides a seeded permutation of the labeled indices into $K=\\min(5,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor)$ outer folds. For each held-out fold, its complement supplies all calibration and selection data. Three inner validation folds are shared across the candidate families. The zero control provides an anchor; the affine fit learns a scalar least-squares slope and intercept; the nonlinear fit uses a linear term, a quadratic term, and positive-part terms with normalized knots $-0.5,0,0.5$. Training-score quantiles determine normalization. The diagonal ridge penalties are $(0.1,2,4,4,4)$. Binary calibration predictions, before the multiplier below, are clipped to $[0,1]$.\n    82\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/program.py#L69-L211]]\n    83\t\n    84\tFor a fitted calibration $q$ and $\\rho=n/N$, its training-only multiplier is\n    85\t\\begin{equation}\n    86\t b=\\frac{\\widehat\\Cov(Y,q(P))}{(1+\\rho)\\widehat\\Var(q(P))},\n    87\t \\qquad g(p)=bq(p).\n    88\t \\label{eq:weight}\n    89\t\\end{equation}\n    90\tDegenerate or nonfinite denominators give the zero control. Multipliers can be signed; clipping $q$ does not restrict $g$ to a probability. On inner validation fold $v$, the criterion is\n    91\t\\begin{equation}\n    92\t J_v(g)=s_v^2\\{Y-g(P)\\}+\\rho s_v^2\\{g(P)\\}.\n    93\t \\label{eq:risk}\n    94\t\\end{equation}\n    95\tStarting from zero, the implementation compares affine and nonlinear controls in increasing complexity. A challenger replaces the retained family only when the mean paired excess risk plus its standard error across the inner folds is negative. The selected family is refitted on the entire outer training complement. This is a conservative heuristic relative to selecting the smallest observed risk, not a calibrated significance test: inner training sets overlap, and three folds do not supply independent risk replicates. The unlabeled score values enter neither normalization nor fitting nor this selection rule; only their sample size enters $\\rho$.\n    96\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/program.py#L154-L211]]\n    97\t\n    98\t\\subsection{Held-out correction and shared-pool studentization}\n    99\tFor outer fold $\\mathcal I_k$, write $n_k=|\\mathcal I_k|$, $w_k=n_k/n$, and let $g_k$ be its fitted control. Define $r_i=Y_i-g_k(P_i)$ for $i\\in\\mathcal I_k$ and $H(u)=\\sum_k w_kg_k(u)$. The estimate and estimated variance are\n   100\t\\begin{align}\n   101\t \\widehat\\theta&=\\sum_k w_k\\overline r_k+\\overline H_U,\\label{eq:crossfit}\\\\\n   102\t \\widehat V&=\\sum_k\\frac{w_k^2}{n_k}s_k^2(r)\n   103\t                        +\\frac{s_U^2(H)}{N}.\\label{eq:studentizer}\n   104\t\\end{align}\n   105\tHere $s^2$ denotes sample variance with the usual degrees-of-freedom correction. The unlabeled term is the variance of a weighted \\emph{sum} of predictions evaluated on the same observations. Treating foldwise unlabeled means as independent would drop their covariance. Centering residuals within folds also avoids treating fold-specific prediction offsets as between-observation residual variation.\n   106\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/program.py#L215-L267]]\n   107\t\n   108\tFor the nonfallback return, the radius is $t_{1-\\alpha/2,\\,\\max(2,n-6)}\\sqrt{\\max(0,\\widehat V)}$. Binary endpoints are clipped to $[0,1]$. Constant scores in either pool trigger label-only inference. For binary outcomes, fewer than ten successes or fewer than ten failures also trigger that fallback: a Clopper--Pearson interval. The real-valued fallback uses a Student interval \\citep{Student}. Its exactness requires Gaussian outcomes, not merely real-valued measurements. These details matter when comparing widths: reference methods need not share the same critical value, variance convention, or fallback behavior.\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/program.py#L270-L305]]\n   110\t\n   111\tAn internal bounded-fold construction is also computed for binary inputs, but does \\emph{not} determine the returned interval. We therefore do not credit its concentration argument to the empirical intervals. Similarly, the presence of an exact binomial fallback does not make the full data-dependent fallback/Wald rule finite-sample exact. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} separates these objects. Readable implementation evidence is included as \\path{source_evidence/program.py}, with the accompanying \\path{source_evidence/METHOD.md}; source locations and the reference implementation are indexed in the supplement.\n   112\t\n   113\t\\section{Variance Rationale and Validity Boundaries}\n   114\t\\subsection{Oracle headroom is conditional}\n   115\tFor a common deterministic $g$, independence gives\n   116\t\\begin{equation}\n   117\t V(g)=\\frac{\\Var\\{Y-g(P)\\}}{n}+\\frac{\\Var\\{g(P)\\}}{N}.\n   118\t\\end{equation}\n   119\tLet $m(P)=\\E[Y\\mid P]$, $e=\\E[\\Var(Y\\mid P)]$, and $a=N/(n+N)$. Orthogonality of $Y-m(P)$ to functions of $P$ and completion of the square yield\n   120\t\\begin{equation}\n   121\t V(g)=\\frac{e}{n}+\\frac{\\Var(m)}{n+N}\n   122\t       +\\left(\\frac1n+\\frac1N\\right)\\Var(g-am).\n   123\t \\label{eq:oracle}\n   124\t\\end{equation}\n   125\tThus $am$, up to an additive constant, minimizes variance within this deterministic control family. The finite-$N$ multiplier is not optional when the unlabeled mean itself has sampling error. This identity does not optimize interval length over arbitrary confidence procedures.\n   126\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L119-L143]]\n   127\t\n   128\tFor $\\Var(P)>0$, the best affine control has variance\n   129\t\\begin{align}\n   130\t V_{\\mathrm{aff}}&=\\frac{\\Var(Y)}n-\n   131\t \\frac{N\\Cov(Y,P)^2}{n(n+N)\\Var(P)},\\\\\n   132\t V_{\\mathrm{aff}}-V_{\\mathrm{oracle}}\n   133\t &=\\frac{N}{n(n+N)}\\left\\{\\Var(m)-\\frac{\\Cov(Y,P)^2}{\\Var(P)}\\right\\}.\\label{eq:headroom}\n   134\t\\end{align}\n   135\tThe gap vanishes for an affine conditional mean, an uninformative score, or a score supported on at most two values. For an independently fixed calibration $\\widehat m$, the corresponding risk identity is\n   136\t\\begin{equation}\n   137\t nV(a\\widehat m)=\\Var(Y)-a\\Var(m)+a\\Var(\\widehat m-m).\n   138\t \\label{eq:calibration}\n   139\t\\end{equation}\n   140\tEven positive nonlinear headroom can be lost to calibration error. These identities motivate the zero and affine anchors, but they neither predict the measured ranking in every condition nor prove that the selected nonlinear basis learns $m$. In particular, an evaluated condition bearing a nonlinear response does not guarantee a width benefit over PPI++.\n   141\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L119-L143]]\n   142\t\n   143\t\\subsection{What the studentizer establishes}\n   144\tFor deterministic, possibly different $g_k$, the exact variance of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:crossfit} is\n   145\t\\begin{equation}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
