\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   160\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L861-L895]]\n   161\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resource_manifest.json#raw_shapes]]\n   162\t\n   163\tThe empirical condition uses the gene-expression example distributed with the fixed PPI reference release, whose source attribution is \\citet{Vaishnav}. Normalized duplicate outcome--score records are grouped before a deterministic development/final partition. Labeled and unlabeled observations are sampled independently with replacement from the final unique-record source. Consequently, its truth is the mean of that empirical source, not a claim about all promoter sequences or a new biological population. Underlying sequence strings are unavailable, so disjointness of normalized scalar records does not establish sequence-level disjointness.\n   164\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resource_manifest.json#source]]\n   165\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L861-L873]]\n   166\t\n   167\tThe remaining information controls use the nonlinear outcomes unchanged. Constant scores replace both prediction pools by $0.5$; permuted scores separately permute the labeled and unlabeled predictions. The latter intervention disrupts label--score pairing while retaining each prediction multiset. These conditions test reliance on information, not isolated contributions of studentization or nonlinear fitting. They share records with the nonlinear condition and must not be counted as independent natural datasets.\n   168\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L885-L893]]\n   169\t\n   170\t\\subsection{References, uncertainty, and assessment scope}\n   171\tThe label-only reference uses exact binomial intervals for binary data and Student intervals for real data. PPI and PPI++ use \\texttt{ppi-python==0.2.3}, with respectively \\texttt{lam=1} and \\texttt{lam=None}. The latter estimates a coefficient clipped to $[0,1]$ and uses pooled prediction variance. On constant scores it uses coefficient zero. Both references return normal-approximation intervals; their finite-sample behavior should not be inferred from asymptotic theory alone.\n   172\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L754-L809]]\n   173\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/ppi.py#L135-L229]]\n   174\t\n   175\tCoverage is computed from recorded interval endpoints and the evaluator's target. The stored coverage intervals are Clopper--Pearson intervals with Bonferroni allocation over seven conditions and four methods, giving joint coverage of at least $95\\%$ under the repetition model. They quantify Monte Carlo uncertainty, not uncertainty about $\\theta$ within a repetition. Dependence between information-control conditions does not invalidate the union bound. Width comparisons use the stored paired bootstrap with 512 seed resamples and random seed 79231. The percentile endpoints use tail probability $0.05/42$ for the seven-by-three comparison family. These are multiplicity-adjusted bootstrap summaries, not exact simultaneous confidence guarantees \\citep{Efron,EfronTibshirani,Dunn}.\n   176\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L920-L974]]\n   177\t\n   178\tWe report the ratio of mean widths, not the mean of per-repetition ratios. This distinction avoids zero-denominator sensitivity. The assessment's secondary aggregate caps per-repetition ratios at 100; we do not interpret that capped aggregate as a typical percentage increase. All final repetitions are retained, with no recorded failures or missing paired intervals. Operational and information-control qualification completed successfully, but the evaluator explicitly does not certify arbitrary theoretical variance assumptions. This completed qualification is not a coverage theorem. Selection used development evidence; final measurements and descriptive source inspection are kept separate from that selection history.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '213,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '213,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"   213\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   214\t\\centering\n   215\t\\begin{subfigure}[t]{\\textwidth}\n   216\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,trim=0 120bp 0 0,clip]{figures/paired_width_comparison.pdf}\n   217\t\\phantomsubcaption\\label{fig:width-a}\n   218\t\\end{subfigure}\\par\n   219\t\\begin{subfigure}[t]{\\textwidth}\n   220\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,trim=0 0 0 146.4bp,clip]{figures/paired_width_comparison.pdf}\n   221\t\\phantomsubcaption\\label{fig:width-b}\n   222\t\\end{subfigure}\n   223\t\\caption{Condition-specific width comparisons. Each point divides frozen-method mean width by the indicated reference mean width; bars are the stored multiplicity-adjusted paired-bootstrap ratio intervals. (a) Predictive conditions. (b) Information controls. Values left of one are narrower, not necessarily more valid. Only gene expression is narrower than PPI++; all six other conditions favor PPI++ on mean width. The rare-outcome comparison requires the coverage and zero-width caveats in the text.}\n   224\t\\label{fig:width}\n   225\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/summary]]\n   226\t\\end{figure}\n   227\t\n   228\t\\subsection{Non-benefits against the tuned reference}\n   229\tAgainst PPI++, the frozen method is wider in every non-gene-expression condition. The ratios of means are $1.0269$ for linear, $1.0259$ for nonlinear, $1.1634$ for rare outcomes, $1.0184$ for uninformative scores, $1.0368$ for constant scores, and $1.0189$ for permuted scores. All six stored paired width-difference intervals are strictly positive. The explicitly nonlinear condition is therefore a substantive non-benefit: including a nonlinear basis and positive theoretical headroom does not suffice to improve the measured interval.\n   230\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json]]\n   231\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/summary]]\n   232\t\n   233\tThe baseline distinction is consequential. The frozen method is narrower than label-only inference in the linear, nonlinear, uninformative, and permuted conditions, yet wider than PPI++ in each. It is also narrower than unweighted PPI under uninformative or permuted scores, where applying an untuned score correction can add noise. Neither comparison establishes that the learned procedure extracts useful nonlinear information. Under constant scores it exactly matches the binomial label-only intervals, as the fallback requires. PPI and PPI++ coincide there but are narrower, reflecting different interval constructions rather than predictive information created by a constant score.\n   234\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json]]\n   235\t\n   236\t\\subsection{Coverage uncertainty and the rare-outcome boundary}\n   237\tThe frozen method's condition-wise coverage spans $0.95$ to $0.98$; its equal-condition aggregate is $0.961071$. On gene expression, 383 of 400 intervals cover the empirical-source mean, giving coverage $0.9575$ with stored simultaneous interval $[0.916312,0.982416]$. Every condition's lower bound for the frozen method is below $0.95$. Thus observing at least nominal coverage in these samples does not demonstrate that the underlying coverage probability is at least nominal. Figure~\\ref{fig:coverage} keeps all simultaneous bounds visible rather than using a pass/fail color based only on point estimates.\n   238\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.empirical_coverage]]\n   239\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   240\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json]]\n   241\t\n   242\tRare outcomes make the efficiency/validity distinction particularly concrete. PPI++ has observed coverage $0.92$ with interval $[0.868958,0.956253]$, which includes nominal coverage. Five of its 400 recorded intervals have zero width. The frozen method has observed coverage $0.98$ and mean width $0.071556$, versus $0.061507$ for PPI++. We retain the narrower reference widths but do not call them a valid practical improvement; conversely, the simultaneous interval does not establish a statistically detected PPI++ coverage shortfall. The evidence shows a finite-sample warning, not a proof of either method's coverage guarantee or superiority.\n   243\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   244\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#conditions/rare_outcome]]\n   245\t\n   246\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   247\t\\centering\n   248\t\\begin{subfigure}[t]{\\textwidth}\n   249\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,trim=0 120bp 0 0,clip]{figures/coverage_with_uncertainty.pdf}\n   250\t\\phantomsubcaption\\label{fig:coverage-a}\n   251\t\\end{subfigure}\\par\n   252\t\\begin{subfigure}[t]{\\textwidth}\n   253\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,trim=0 0 0 146.4bp,clip]{figures/coverage_with_uncertainty.pdf}\n   254\t\\phantomsubcaption\\label{fig:coverage-b}\n   255\t\\end{subfigure}\n   256\t\\caption{Coverage and Monte Carlo uncertainty for all compared methods. Bars are the stored Clopper--Pearson intervals adjusted over all seven conditions and four methods; the dashed line is nominal coverage $0.95$. (a) Predictive conditions. (b) Information controls. Lower bounds for the frozen method remain below nominal throughout. In the rare-outcome condition, PPI++ has the lowest observed coverage, but its interval still contains nominal. These measurements are not finite-sample certificates.}\n   257\t\\label{fig:coverage}\n   258\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   259\t\\end{figure}\n   260\t\n   261\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   262\t\\paragraph{What the comparison answers.}\n   263\tHolding all observation resources fixed leaves room to change how a score is used. The gene-expression result demonstrates a measured width reduction for the complete frozen procedure relative to the supplied references. The non-benefits show why that observation cannot be promoted to a generally better method. In particular, comparisons with an untuned correction can exaggerate the apparent value of learning a richer calibration. The tuned reference is indispensable for asking whether extra flexibility actually improves interval efficiency.\n   264\t\n   265\t\\paragraph{Validity remains a separate requirement.}\n   266\tThe original goal requires nominal coverage, not simply narrower intervals. The present evidence does not fully discharge that requirement. Deterministic-control variance identities are exact under their assumptions, while the learned-fold argument additionally requires stability and moments. The returned interval is asymptotic, its fallback is selected using the observed outcomes, and a separately computed bounded construction is not its certificate. The completed final qualification confirms the assessment's operational conditions, not these statistical assumptions. We therefore present a conditional efficiency study with an unresolved finite-sample validity boundary, rather than a generally valid interval-shortening solution.\n   267\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   268\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L144-L183]]\n   269\t\n   270\t\\paragraph{Identification and external validity.}\n   271\tThere is no controlled component ablation, so neither the role of the nonlinear basis nor the benefit of offset invariance is empirically isolated. The information controls alter the prediction information, not a single inference component. Only one real-valued empirical source is evaluated. Sampling normalized records with replacement gives a well-defined empirical-source estimand, but not a population-level biological validation or sequence-level independence guarantee. Heavy tails, distribution shift, informative labeling, predictor reuse of these labels, alternative sample sizes, and other scientific targets were not evaluated. The synthetic archives permit exact input replay, but the complete generating functions are not provided in the available manifest; we do not reconstruct them from condition names.\n   272\t\n   273\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty and reproducibility.}\n   274\tThe simultaneous binomial intervals are intentionally conservative. The paired bootstrap summarizes the completed repetitions, but its extreme percentile endpoints use a limited resampling budget; it should not be read as an exact simultaneous test. Multiple comparisons and estimator selection are different issues: adjusting result summaries does not validate the inner model-selection rule. Replacing Bonferroni allocation with a stepwise procedure such as \\citet{Holm} would change the reporting protocol and is not evaluated here. Finally, the upstream example archive has no separately declared data license in the supplied provenance. Reproduction from available local resources is documented, but redistribution rights require resolution before public release.\n   275\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resource_manifest.json#source/license]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
