{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '277,344p'\"","aggregated_output":"   277\t\n   278\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   279\tRisk-adaptive frozen-score controls provide a concrete way to investigate interval efficiency without obtaining more labels, changing a predictor, or enlarging the unlabeled sample. Their variance target clarifies both the potential value of nonlinear calibration and the cost of estimating it. Accounting for a shared prediction pool and preserving fold-offset invariance are useful implementation properties, but neither implies universal narrowing or finite-sample coverage.\n   280\t\n   281\tThe completed matched assessment supports a localized empirical-source result: narrower gene-expression intervals than both PPI references, accompanied by wider intervals than PPI++ in every other evaluated condition. Coverage measurements remain uncertain, and the rare-outcome reference exposes degenerate intervals that prevent a width-only interpretation. The study therefore answers the fixed-resource question conditionally rather than affirmatively in general. Establishing a generally valid shortening rule would require a returned certificate or a fully verified asymptotic regime, stronger coverage evidence, and controlled comparisons separating calibration from studentization. Those are unresolved requirements, not outcomes claimed here.\n   282\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   283\t\n   284\t\\clearpage\n   285\t\\bibliography{references}\n   286\t\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n   287\t\\clearpage\n   288\t\\appendix\n   289\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   290\t\\subsection{Derivations and the scope of exact identities}\n   291\tFor a deterministic control, write $Y-g(P)=Y-m(P)+m(P)-g(P)$. Conditional mean zero of $Y-m(P)$ makes its covariance with every square-integrable function of $P$ vanish. Hence\n   292\t\\[\n   293\t nV(g)=e+\\Var(m-g)+\\frac nN\\Var(g).\n   294\t\\]\n   295\tCompleting the square gives Eq.~\\eqref{eq:oracle}; optimizing over an affine slope gives Eq.~\\eqref{eq:headroom}. Equation~\\eqref{eq:calibration} follows by substituting $g=a\\widehat m$. Additive constants are immaterial throughout. These calculations explain a restricted oracle comparison, not an efficiency lower bound over all possible inference procedures. In particular, binary scores support only an affine function of the score, and a constant score offers no nonconstant control in this class.\n   296\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L108-L143]]\n   297\t\n   298\tFor independently fixed fold controls, the unlabeled term in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:fixedfold} expands as\n   299\t\\[\n   300\t \\frac1N\\sum_{k,\\ell}w_kw_\\ell\\Cov\\{g_k(P),g_\\ell(P)\\}.\n   301\t\\]\n   302\tAll cross terms are retained by evaluating $H$ before taking its variance. For learned controls, exact unbiasedness of the full estimator can be checked fold by fold under integrability and the stated training boundary, but the deterministic variance expression is not thereby an exact conditional variance formula. Cross-fold labeled dependence remains. A stable common-control limit, rather than joint conditional independence, is the route described in the method note.\n   303\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/METHOD.md#L108-L166]]\n   304\t\n   305\t\\subsection{Complete coverage and paired differences}\n   306\tTable~\\ref{tab:coverage} supplies observed coverage for every reference. The uncertainty for these points is shown in the main paper, rather than omitted from an unfavorable comparison. Table~\\ref{tab:diff} supplies absolute paired width-difference intervals, preserving both the empirical-source benefit and every non-benefit against PPI++.\n   307\t\n   308\t\\begin{table}[h]\n   309\t\\centering\n   310\t\\caption{Observed coverage over 400 repetitions per condition. All entries are computed directly from the recorded endpoints and target. These point estimates do not certify nominal coverage.}\n   311\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n   312\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n   313\t\\toprule\n   314\tCondition & Frozen & Label only & PPI & PPI++\\\\\n   315\t\\midrule\n   316\tGene expression & .9575 & .9625 & .9550 & .9500\\\\\n   317\tLinear & .9575 & .9650 & .9475 & .9525\\\\\n   318\tNonlinear & .9500 & .9700 & .9475 & .9500\\\\\n   319\tRare outcome & .9800 & .9750 & .9600 & .9200\\\\\n   320\tUninformative & .9575 & .9675 & .9550 & .9550\\\\\n   321\tConstant score & .9700 & .9700 & .9525 & .9525\\\\\n   322\tPermuted score & .9550 & .9700 & .9850 & .9575\\\\\n   323\t\\bottomrule\n   324\t\\end{tabular}\n   325\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json]]\n   326\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   327\t\\end{table}\n   328\t\n   329\t\\begin{table}[h]\n   330\t\\centering\n   331\t\\caption{Stored paired-bootstrap intervals for mean width differences, frozen method minus PPI++. Positive values favor the reference on width only. No coverage qualification is encoded in the sign.}\n   332\t\\label{tab:diff}\n   333\t\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n   334\t\\toprule\n   335\tCondition & Lower endpoint & Upper endpoint\\\\\n   336\t\\midrule\n   337\tGene expression & $-.029691$ & $-.024356$\\\\\n   338\tLinear & $.003664$ & $.004625$\\\\\n   339\tNonlinear & $.003299$ & $.004241$\\\\\n   340\tRare outcome & $.009753$ & $.010365$\\\\\n   341\tUninformative & $.002890$ & $.003532$\\\\\n   342\tConstant score & $.006128$ & $.006150$\\\\\n   343\tPermuted score & $.002856$ & $.003529$\\\\\n   344\t\\bottomrule\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
