{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat source_evidence/METHOD.md; cat references_provenance.json | head -100; sed -n '1,160p' REPRODUCIBILITY.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Offset-invariant risk-adaptive controls: implementation note\n\nThis is a source-only candidate, not an evaluated research paper. No candidate\ncode, tests, simulations, package managers, network requests, or evaluators were\nrun during this stage. No measured coverage, width, score, or runtime is claimed.\nOnly the supplied task and permitted starter files were read; there was no\nliterature browsing or forbidden-content encounter. The larger paper/PDF,\nofficial template/checklist, real-data experiments, and repeated-run evidence\nremain unperformed, not replaced by illustrative numbers.\n\n## Interface, target, and scope\n\nThe target is the superpopulation mean theta = E[Y]. Inputs are labeled outcomes\ny[n], their frozen scalar predictions p[n], and independent unlabeled predictions\nu[N]. Assume iid labeled pairs and iid unlabeled scores from the same population,\nindependent of each other, with an externally frozen predictor. Conditioning on\none realized input fixes resources across methods; coverage still concerns\nrepeated sampling, not uncertainty conditional on all those realized values.\nInformative label selection, distribution shift, and predictor training on these\nlabels are outside this justification.\n\nThe reset/start/chunk/commit/infer protocol, validation, response keys/types,\ntransfer format, manifest and entrypoint are unchanged. Only infer returns\n`lower` and `upper`; no diagnostics or additional observations enter responses.\nReset clears fits and seeded random state. Training and selection use only outer\ntraining labels/scores and the supplied sample sizes; unlabeled values never\nenter training, selection, normalization, or multiplier fitting. No X, oracle\nlabels, baseline intervals, hidden inputs, or cross-episode memory are assumed.\n\n## Implemented selection and fitting\n\n`program.py::_crossfit_controls` partitions a reset-seeded permutation into\nK=min(5,floor(n/2)) held-out folds. Each has at least two observations for the\nsupported n>=8. Explicit index assertions verify the outer training boundary.\nEach outer training sample uses three inner folds (at least two validation\nobservations per fold at the smallest supported size). All families use exactly\nthe same inner splits, drawn before fitting any family.\n\nThe prespecified families, in increasing complexity, are:\n\n1. Zero control.\n2. Affine least-squares calibration of the scalar score. Score normalization,\n   intercept, and slope are fitted only on the relevant training sample. For\n   binary outcomes the calibration prediction is clipped to [0,1].\n3. The starter's small spline/ridge calibration: linear, quadratic and three\n   positive-part terms at normalized knots -0.5, 0 and 0.5. Training-only\n   10th/90th percentile scaling, range fallback, clipped features, and penalties\n   (0.1,2,4,4,4) are retained. Binary predictions are clipped to [0,1].\n\nFor each fitted nonzero calibration q, let rho=n/N using the full supplied\nsizes, even during inner fitting. Fit\n\n    b = Cov_train(Y,q(P)) / [(1+rho) Var_train(q(P))],\n    g(p) = b*q(p).\n\nNonfinite or machine-resolution-degenerate denominators, or nonfinite weights,\nproduce a zero control. The relative variance guard is measured against the\nsquared maximum training calibration magnitude. It is a numerical guard, not\na performance tuning parameter. The signed multiplier is applied exactly once;\nthere is no additional multiplication by N/(n+N) after fitting g. Binary q is\nclipped BEFORE multiplication, not after multiplying a potentially signed b.\n\nOn each inner validation fold compute\n\n    J_hat(g) = sample_var(Y-g(P)) + rho*sample_var(g(P)).\n\nStart from zero. Compare affine, then nonlinear to the currently selected\nfamily. Move only if the mean paired excess risk plus its foldwise sample\nstandard deviation divided by sqrt(number of inner folds) is negative. Refit\nboth calibration and multiplier on all outer training data after selection.\nValidation folds are equally weighted for this prespecified heuristic even\nwhen sizes differ by one. Because fits overlap, this foldwise quantity is NOT\na calibrated standard error, a significance test, or a coverage guarantee.\nNo completed intervals are compared or selected for their realized widths.\n\n## Offset-invariant shared-pool studentization\n\nFor fold k of size n_k and weight w_k=n_k/n, let g_k be its fitted control,\nr_ki=Y_i-g_k(P_i), and H(u)=sum_k w_k*g_k(u). The actual return path uses\n\n    theta_hat = sum_k w_k*mean(r_k) + mean(H(u)),\n    V_L = sum_k w_k^2*sample_var(r_k)/n_k,\n    V_U = sample_var(H(u))/N,\n    V_hat = V_L + V_U.\n\nVariance is computed only AFTER combining all controls on the same unlabeled\npool. This retains every covariance term from shared observations; a sum of\nfold-specific unlabeled variances would generally be wrong.\n\nReplacing each g_k by g_k+c_k changes r_k by -c_k and H by the constant\nsum_k w_k*c_k. Both the point estimate and variance are unchanged, including\nunequal fold sizes. The same center/variance implies identical endpoints under\nthe same interval builder. These shifted controls are abstract signed controls,\nnot modified predictor outputs; they must not be clipped again after shifting.\nPooled residual sample variance need not have this invariance. This algebra\nalone implies neither improved coverage nor a decrease in measured width.\nWithin-fold variance can be larger or smaller on individual realized samples.\n\n`_interval_from_statistics` retains the starter's Student-t critical value with\nmax(2,n-6) degrees of freedom. Binary endpoints are clipped to [0,1]. Constant\nscores in either pool and binary samples with fewer than ten successes or\nfailures retain the label-only rule: Clopper-Pearson for binary, Student-t for\nreal. Real Student-t intervals are exact under iid Gaussian outcomes, not\ngeneral real outcomes. The data-dependent binary fallback does not give the\ncombined Wald/fallback procedure an exact finite-sample coverage guarantee.\n\n## Variance identities and their limits\n\nFor deterministic fold controls, independent sampling gives exactly\n\n    E[theta_hat] = theta,\n    Var(theta_hat) = sum_k w_k^2*Var(Y-g_k(P))/n_k\n                    + Var(sum_k w_k*g_k(P))/N.\n\nThe labeled terms are independent because deterministic functions are applied\nto disjoint iid observations, and the unlabeled sample is independent of them.\nThe implemented sample-variance estimate is unbiased for this deterministic\nvariance, even when controls have different shapes. Normal or t coverage is\nstill not exact merely because variance is estimated without bias.\n\nFor a common deterministic control g, completing the square with\nm(P)=E[Y|P], e=E[Var(Y|P)], and a=N/(n+N) gives\n\n    V(g) = e/n + Var(m)/(n+N) + (1/n+1/N)*Var(g-a*m).\n\nFor an independently fixed calibration m_hat and J(g)=n*V(g), this yields\n\n    J(a*m_hat) = Var(Y) - a*Var(m) + a*Var(m_hat-m).\n\nThus calibration estimation error can consume the available prediction\nheadroom. At the oracle level, the optimal affine variance and nonlinear gap\nfor positive score variance are\n\n    V_affine = Var(Y)/n - N*Cov(Y,P)^2/[n*(n+N)*Var(P)],\n    V_affine - V_oracle = N/[n*(n+N)]\n                         *[Var(m)-Cov(Y,P)^2/Var(P)].\n\nThe gap is zero for affine conditional means, uninformative scores, and scores\nwith at most two support points. Estimated nonlinear fits are not guaranteed\nto improve on a fitted affine or zero control. These are deterministic oracle\nidentities, not a learned-nuisance coverage theorem or a universal lower bound\nover arbitrary confidence intervals.\n\nFor learned cross-fit controls, overlapping training folds create dependence:\nthe deterministic variance formula is NOT an exact conditional-on-training\nformula for the full estimator. A sufficient asymptotic framework is fixed K,\nn,N tending to infinity with N/n tending to a positive finite constant,\nfinite 2+delta moments with suitable uniform integrability for fitted residual\nand control squares, nondegenerate limiting variance, and stable selection and\nfitting so that g_k-c_k converges in L2 to one common deterministic g for every\nfold. The c_k may be training-dependent additive constants. They cancel exactly\nbefore the usual cross-fit argument. Conditional on the training observations\nfor a given fold, the centered held-out nuisance-error average is negligible\nat n^(-1/2) when its L2 norm tends to zero; the independent shared unlabeled\nsample is handled by the analogous average for the combined nuisance. Fixed\nK then gives the common-control first-order limit. The within-fold residual\nvariances and shared-pool variance consistently estimate its variance under\nthe stated moment/stability conditions, giving asymptotic Wald coverage.\n\nThese conditions need not hold for arbitrary unstable nuisance fits or\nnear-tied selections among distinct limiting controls. The one-standard-error\nrule does not establish them. A small basis need not converge to m. Heavy\ntails, rare binary events, unstable selection, and small samples require actual\ncoverage checks; there is no universal finite-sample t guarantee here.\n\n## Separate internal bounded certificate\n\nAs in the starter, bounded inputs also compute a standalone internal certificate\non every input, before choosing a fallback or Wald interval. If a fitted binary\ncontrol has weight b, it lies in [L,U]=[min(0,b),max(0,b)], since its calibration\nq lies in [0,1]. Its residual lies in [-U,1-L]. Bounds are training-only and\nremain valid for zero and signed controls. The original two-sided empirical\nBernstein radius is retained for each fold's residual and control means, with\nerror allocation alpha/(2K), weighted addition and a union bound. The sample\nvariance, range term and truncation are the starter's unchanged implementation.\nConditional independence within each held-out/unlabeled sample and a union\nbound suffice; no independence of reused pools or overlapping folds is claimed.\nThe weighted center is the same as the reported estimator, but this certificate\ndoes NOT determine the reported interval and is not exposed by RPC. The return\nmust not be described as certified.\n\n## Source-tree tests and deferred experiments\n\n`test_controls.py` contains executable unittest regression checks for the actual\n`_control_statistics` and `_interval_from_statistics` used in inference. They\ninject five unequal offsets AFTER fitting, into both held-out and shared-pool\ncontrols, for equal and unequal fold sizes, without reclipping. Other tests\ncheck the explicit shared-pool covariance formula, zero/constant controls,\nsingle-use finite-N weighting, identical inner splits and full-training refit,\nouter training-only inputs, small samples, finite bounded endpoints,\nconstant-score fallback, response keys, state transitions, and reset replay.\nThey were authored but NOT executed. When execution is authorized, from the\ncandidate directory the focused command is:\n\n    python -m unittest discover -s src -p 'test_controls.py'\n\nNo new dependency is introduced: implementation uses existing NumPy and SciPy;\ntests additionally use the Python standard library. Dependency versions and\nruntime have not been measured or pinned in this non-executing stage.\n\nRequired future scientific checks include paired repeated draws for incumbent,\nvariance-only, affine-only, selection-only and combined methods, plus label-only,\nPPI, tuned PPI++ and a permitted fixed-stratum comparator. Cover affine and\nnonlinear signals, weak/shuffled scores, rare events, heavy tails, unequal\nfolds, and varying N/n; use identical n,N and frozen predictions across methods.\nReport coverage uncertainty and paired width uncertainty. Real-table studies\nmust avoid distribution-shift pooling. The interface has no population labels\nor oracle means, so such diagnostics cannot be invented from seeds or presumed\nevaluator inputs. No empirical improvement, nominal coverage, completed paper,\nor publication artifact is asserted by this candidate.\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:15.599928+00:00\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"Prediction-powered inference\",\n    \"PPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference\",\n    \"Cross-prediction-powered inference\",\n    \"Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters\",\n    \"The evolution, evolvability and engineering of gene regulatory DNA\",\n    \"The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the binomial\",\n    \"The probable error of a mean\",\n    \"Interval estimation for a binomial proportion\",\n    \"Bootstrap methods: Another look at the jackknife\",\n    \"An Introduction to the Bootstrap\",\n    \"Probability inequalities for sums of bounded random variables\",\n    \"Empirical Bernstein Bounds and Sample Variance Penalization\",\n    \"Multiple comparisons among means\",\n    \"A Simple Sequentially Rejective Multiple Test Procedure\",\n    \"Some results on generalized difference estimation and generalized regression estimation for finite populations\",\n    \"Model Assisted Survey Sampling\",\n    \"Empirical efficiency maximization: improved locally efficient covariate adjustment in randomized experiments and survival analysis\",\n    \"Semi-supervised inference: General theory and estimation of means\",\n    \"Some new perspectives on the method of control variates\",\n    \"The Elements of Statistical Learning\",\n    \"Ridge regression: Biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\",\n    \"Cross-validatory choice and assessment of statistical predictions\",\n    \"Stacked generalization\",\n    \"Stacked regressions\",\n    \"Inference for the generalization error\"\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W4388520288\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W4388520288\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"PPI\",\n      \"title\": \"Prediction-powered inference\",\n      \"year\": 2023,\n      \"query\": \"Prediction-powered inference\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adi6000\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:13.072953+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_file\": \"reference_evidence/PPI.txt\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Exact-work metadata and available abstract only; no citation graph followed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2311.01453\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01453\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"PPIPlus\",\n      \"title\": \"PPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference\",\n      \"year\": 2023,\n      \"query\": \"PPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01453\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:12.921616+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_file\": \"reference_evidence/PPIPlus.txt\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Exact-work metadata and available abstract only; no citation graph followed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2309.16598\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16598\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"CrossPPI\",\n      \"title\": \"Cross-Prediction-Powered Inference\",\n      \"year\": 2023,\n      \"query\": \"Cross-prediction-powered inference\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16598\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:12.940958+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_file\": \"reference_evidence/CrossPPI.txt\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Exact-work metadata and available abstract only; no citation graph followed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W3123436326\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W3123436326\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"DML\",\n      \"title\": \"Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters\",\n      \"year\": 2017,\n      \"query\": \"Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1111/ectj.12097\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:13.135345+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_file\": \"reference_evidence/DML.txt\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Exact-work metadata and available abstract only; no citation graph followed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W4221128536\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W4221128536\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Vaishnav\",\n      \"title\": \"The evolution, evolvability and engineering of gene regulatory DNA\",\n      \"year\": 2022,\n      \"query\": \"The evolution, evolvability and engineering of gene regulatory DNA\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-04506-6\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:13.201405+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_file\": \"reference_evidence/Vaishnav.txt\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Exact-work metadata and available abstract only; no citation graph followed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W2137591261\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W2137591261\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"ClopperPearson\",\n      \"title\": \"THE USE OF CONFIDENCE OR FIDUCIAL LIMITS ILLUSTRATED IN THE CASE OF THE BINOMIAL\",\n      \"year\": 1934,\n      \"query\": \"The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the binomial\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/26.4.404\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T20:18:13.487783+00:00\",\n# Reproduction and evidence boundaries\n\n## Scope\n\nThe manuscript analyzes an existing, completed evaluation. No new scientific\nreplications, benchmark evaluations, or controlled ablations were run while\nwriting it. `paper_data.json` is read-only and is the authoritative quantitative\nregistry. The final outcome is **qualified**, independently of the absent\n`downstream_study`; this is operational qualification, not scientific coverage\ncertification. The conservative scientific-validity field does not diagnose a\ncoverage or adaptivity failure. All six non-benefits against PPI++ are retained.\n\nThe selected source is copied into `source_evidence/`. Its identity is associated\nwith the selected development record, but the final frozen tree hash has not\nbeen independently reconstructed. No byte-identity claim is made. The method\ndescription is grounded in readable source, not hashes or prior audit verdicts.\n\n## Artifact-only build (performed)\n\nAuxiliary Python packages are installed outside this workspace, in\n`/tmp/paper-tools`. The figure environment uses Python 3.12, NumPy 1.26.4,\nSciPy 1.12.0, and Matplotlib 3.8.4. Optional validation uses jsonschema and\nPyMuPDF. To recreate this environment without polluting the manuscript:\n<!-- @evidence[[source_evidence/requirements.txt]] -->\n<!-- @evidence[[build_artifacts.py]] -->\n\n```sh\npython -m venv /tmp/paper-tools\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/pip install numpy==1.26.4 scipy==1.12.0 matplotlib==3.8.4 jsonschema pymupdf\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/python build_artifacts.py\npython build_claims.py\npython record_artifact_provenance.py\nbash .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir .\npython .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py --tex paper.tex --bib references.bib\npython .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py --paper-data paper_data.json --references references_provenance.json --bib references.bib --claims claims_grounding.json --paper-dir .\nbash .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\n\nThe figure build requires no additional experiment and uses the stored\nuncertainty intervals rather than generating new bootstraps. It verifies every\nstored mean width against the 400 paired endpoint records. Runtime for this\nartifact-only build is short; no prospective runtime guarantee is asserted.\nActual timing, compilation and validation status are recorded in\n`validation_report.json` after verification.\n\nTwo canonical wrapper limitations were encountered: the compile wrapper rejects\na system TeX symlink into another approved TeX root, and the figure validator\nimports an unstaged `src.orchestrator` module. Sanitized direct LaTeX/BibTeX\ncommands, a resolved-path dependency audit, the canonical figure JSON schema,\nstrategy density budgets, and the rendered-SI validator were checked instead.\nThe wrappers themselves were not modified. `audit_artifacts.py` records these\nlimitations and independently verifies the nine-body-page PDF, citation keys,\nstandalone language, figure paths/budgets and evidence report. This independent\naudit is not represented as a pass of the unavailable orchestrator validators.\n\n### Figures and tables\n\n- Main Table 1: `build_artifacts.py` produces `analysis_summary.json` from final\n  endpoints; the rounded table is embedded in `paper.tex`.\n- Coverage and width-difference supplementary tables: same script and existing\n  first-episode `solution.summary` uncertainty records.\n- Figure `paired_width_comparison`: `build_artifacts.py`, vector PDF and 300-DPI PNG.\n- Figure `coverage_with_uncertainty`: same script and formats.\n- Figure `fixed_resource_overview`: orchestrator-owned conceptual rendering\n  following the strategy, not an experimental measurement. The draft contains\n  an explicitly temporary placeholder and `{}` prompt-log stub. The build script\n  is self-guarding: it skips an existing image, nonempty prompt log or completion\n  marker. It cannot overwrite the orchestrator's rendering. No paid image-model\n  enhancement is authorized. The final orchestrator is responsible for replacing\n  this placeholder and recompiling.\n\nEvery derived quantitative artifact has an adjacent `.prov.json`. Inline\nevidence references are LaTeX comments so they are machine-checkable without\nappearing as internal metadata in the rendered manuscript.\n\n## Existing experimental code and resources (not rerun)\n\n`source_evidence/program.py` is the selected CPU-only JSON-line inference entrypoint.\nIt accepts labels, their frozen scores and unlabeled scores; it has no training\nor data acquisition API. `source_evidence/test_controls.py` provides algebra and\ninterface unit tests. These were not run during writing. A focused test command\nis `python -m unittest discover -s source_evidence -p test_controls.py`.\n\n`source_evidence/evaluate_final.py` preserves the evaluator. The complete local\ntask is available under `task/`. The original declared dependencies are preserved\nin `source_evidence/requirements.txt`; this is a declaration, not a claim that\nall its versions were independently resolved in the artifact-only environment.\nThe evaluator expects its original task directory and worker setup, not a copy\nwith only the entrypoint moved. A full replay command, **not executed here**, is:\n\n```sh\npython task/evaluate_final.py \\\n  --program_path \"$PWD/source_evidence/program.py\" \\\n  --results_dir /tmp/replayed-final-results\n```\n\nUse the original task execution environment for this command; retain\n`task/resources/final/`, its manifest, and its worker dependencies. The final\narchives are already present locally and need not be downloaded to replay the\nrecorded inputs. The preserved final manifest reports 3340.755 seconds elapsed\nfor the original evaluation; this is not a newly measured writing-stage runtime\nor a guarantee on another CPU. Source tests and replay can be run independently;\nneither is claimed as completed in this paper-writing step.\n<!-- @evidence[[source_evidence/final_manifest.json#compute_usage/elapsed_seconds]] -->\n\n### Data versions and acquisition\n\n`source_evidence/resource_manifest.json` records the exact hashes, sampling,\nnormalization, seed list, and empirical-source origin. The final data are at\n`task/resources/final/{linear,nonlinear,uninformative,rare_outcome,gene_expression}.npz`.\nSynthetic draws are archived for seeds 1000--1399; the stored generation\ndescription states uniform scalar scores and Bernoulli outcomes but does not\nsupply all conditional functions. Replaying the archived inputs is supported;\nreconstructing missing functions from condition names is not.\n\nThe upstream gene-expression example comes from `ppi-python==0.2.3`, repository\nrevision `6b8475b53f0303e4abc4d1eb0b94aa8052f9b940`. Acquisition in an original,\nseparate environment is:\n\n```python\nfrom ppi_py.datasets import load_dataset\ndata = load_dataset('./data/', 'gene_expression')\n```\n\nThis resolves the Google Drive URL recorded in the manifest. The downloaded\narchive checksum is\n`72565d2d88ed6a16331b3423da5dee036e97f2220fad054eb139ced1942e3303`.\nThe existing final unique-record table, not this full upstream archive, defines\nthe empirical-source target. Normalization and duplicate grouping precede a\nhash-sorted development/final split. The precise original preprocessing code is\nnot supplied in this workspace; no replacement reconstruction is claimed.\nUnderlying promoter strings are not available. Independent data redistribution\nrights are undeclared; do not infer them from the code's MIT license.\n<!-- @evidence[[source_evidence/resource_manifest.json]] -->\n\n### Randomness and uncertainty\n\n- Final statistical seeds: 1000--1399, 400 repetitions per condition.\n- Synthetic resource RNG: PCG64, `SeedSequence([seed, case_index, 17923])` as\n  documented in the resource manifest.\n- Empirical sampling: `SeedSequence([seed, 4, 17923])`, with replacement.\n- Permutation control: `SeedSequence([seed, 991])`, separate permutations in\n  the two prediction pools; outcomes unchanged.\n- Inference seed: for each condition, the evaluator cycles through the first\n  64 statistical seeds (1000--1063), using `reset_seeds[index % 64]` for\n  repetition index 0--399. Selection uses outer and inner permutations. This\n  reset seed is distinct from the statistical sampling seed after index 63.\n  No cross-episode state is retained.\n- Coverage intervals: Clopper--Pearson with tails `0.05/(7*4*2)`.\n- Width intervals: existing paired bootstrap, 512 resamples, RNG 79231, tail\n  `0.05/42`. This budget gives limited extreme-tail resolution. We do not\n  describe the percentile intervals as exact simultaneous confidence sets.\n<!-- @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L920-L974]] -->\n<!-- @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1001-L1023]] -->\n\n## Literature and venue\n\n`retrieve_screened_references.py` retrieves exact permitted works and dated\nhistorical foundations only. It uses direct DOI lookups or exact-title lookup;\narXiv metadata overrides ambiguous bibliographic search matches for the three\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
